.\" Man page for hive-zfs
.TH HIVE-ZFS 1 "September 2026" "DruidOS 0.1.0" "DruidOS Hive Stack"

.SH NAME
hive-zfs \- housekeeping of the Hive's ZFS safety snapshots

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hive-zfs prune

.SH DESCRIPTION
DruidOS installs onto ZFS. Before a package changes -
.BR hive-apt (1)
install, remove, upgrade or autoremove, or a package action approved in
.BR hive-dashboard (1)
- the system datasets (the ones mounted at / and /boot) are snapshotted
recursively, as
.IR dataset @hive-before- change - YYYYmmdd - HHMMSS
(UTC). If that snapshot can't be taken, the change doesn't happen.
.PP
.B hive-zfs prune
keeps those snapshots from piling up. It is run daily, as root, by
.BR hive-zfs-prune.timer .

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B prune
For each system dataset, destroy (recursively) its
.B hive-*
snapshots older than
.B prune_after_days
days, except the
.B keep_newest
most recent ones, whatever their age. A snapshot's age is read from the
timestamp in its name. Snapshots not named by the Hive are never touched.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hive/config.toml
.B [zfs]
.B prune_after_days
(default 14) and
.B keep_newest
(default 3).
.B [actuator] snapshot_dataset
replaces the detected system datasets with one dataset.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/hive-zfs-prune.timer
The daily schedule.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
The Hive's snapshots:
.B zfs list -t snapshot -o name,creation | grep @hive-
.TP
Prune now:
.B sudo systemctl start hive-zfs-prune.service

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zfs (8),
.BR hive-apt (1)
